<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jamestown</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jamestown was an English settlement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> founded in 1607. It was the first permanent English colony in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>. Its survival marks a key step in the foundation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> overseas.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jamestown.jpg" alt="Jamestown" width="320" height="358" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown (rediscovered by Alexander Brown) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jamestownzuniga.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The first permanent English settlement in the Americas came at <b>Jamestown</b>, Virginia, in 1607, a colony so precarious that the great majority of its settlers died during the “Starving Time” of 1609–1610.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>From the late sixteenth century overseas trade expanded through chartered companies such as the East India Company, founded in 1600, and through colonies in North America and the Caribbean, beginning with <b>Jamestown</b> in Virginia in 1607.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>When the Virginia Company founded <b>Jamestown</b> in May 1607, Wahunsenacah first hoped to make the English his tributaries; in December 1607 a hunting party under Opechancanough captured John Smith and brought him to Werowocomoco.</blockquote><a href="/empire/powhatan-confederacy">Powhatan Confederacy · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The United States grew out of thirteen British colonies along the Atlantic coast of North America, founded between the settlement of <b>Jamestown</b> in Virginia in 1607 and the chartering of Georgia in 1732.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-states">United States · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1607 the English at <b>Jamestown</b> met Wahunsenacawh, known as Powhatan, paramount chief of some thirty peoples of Tidewater Virginia.</blockquote><a href="/government/chiefdom">Chiefdom · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>The London company founded <b>Jamestown</b> the following year.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-10">10 April in history</a></li></ul></section>
